; Vec - a growable array of machine words, in Axiom.
;
; A `Vec` is the address of a three-word header:
;
;     word 0 : len  - number of live elements
;     word 1 : cap  - elements the data block can hold
;     word 2 : data - address of cap * 8 bytes of element storage
;
; Elements are machine words. Because Axiom's representation is uniform -
; every value is one 64-bit word - the same `Vec` code stores `Int`s,
; `Str` handles, and boxed `data` values with no monomorphisation, which
; is why the element type is `Int` rather than a type parameter: a
; parameter would buy type safety the caller does not get anyway once it
; puts a `Str` handle (also an `Int`) in.
;
; The header is mutated in place, so a `Vec` handle is stable for the
; lifetime of the vector: only `data` is ever reallocated, and it lives
; behind the header. That is the property `Intern` depends on, and it is
; why the mutating operations here can be called in statement position.
; They still *return* the handle, so that code written today stays correct
; if the header ever has to move (a shrinking or arena-compacting
; implementation would move it).
;
; LEAK ON GROWTH: there is no `free` - memory comes from the backend's
; `mmap` bump allocator and is reclaimed only at process exit - so
; doubling the data block abandons the old one. Pushing n elements into a
; vector that starts empty therefore touches about 2n words of storage
; and leaves n of them unreachable. For a compiler process that is the
; right trade (see `Mem.memAlloc`); for anything long-running it is not,
; and the fix is an allocator with `free`, not a change here.
(import Mem)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Construction
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The capacity `vecNew` starts with.
;
; Eight, because the vectors a compiler creates most often - an argument
; list, a scope frame, a constructor's field list - are small enough that
; eight never grows, and the header plus eight words is 88 bytes, which
; is a single bump of the allocator. Starting at 1 or 2 would make those
; cases pay two or three reallocations each, and every reallocation leaks
; (see the module comment).
(:: vecDefaultCap Int)

(fn (vecDefaultCap) 8)

; An empty `Vec` with `vecDefaultCap` capacity.
(pub :: vecNew Int)

(pub fn (vecNew) (vecWithCapacity vecDefaultCap))

; An empty `Vec` that can hold at least `cap` elements without growing.
;
; `cap` is clamped up to 1 rather than honoured as 0. A zero capacity
; would be a real trap here: growth is `cap * 2`, and doubling zero is
; zero, so a zero-capacity vector would recurse forever the first time it
; was pushed to. Clamping at the constructor means `vecGrownCap` can rely
; on `cap >= 1` and needs no special case.
(pub :: vecWithCapacity (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecWithCapacity cap)
  (let (
    (n (if (< cap 1)
      1
      cap
    ))
    (v (memAlloc 24))
  )
    {
      (memSetWord v 0 0)
      (memSetWord v 1 n)
      (memSetWord v 2 (memAlloc (* n 8)))
      v
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Accessors
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: vecLen (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecLen v) (memGetWord v 0))

(pub :: vecCap (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecCap v) (memGetWord v 1))

; The address of the element block.
;
; Exposed because `Intern` and any other module that wants to walk a
; vector without paying a bounds check per element needs it, and because
; hiding it would be pretend safety: the handle is an `Int`, so a caller
; that wants the data pointer can already read word 2 itself.
(:: vecData (-> Int Int))

(fn (vecData v) (memGetWord v 2))

; The element at `i`, or 0 when `i` is out of range.
;
; Out of range answers 0 rather than trapping, matching `Str.strByte`:
; the callers that matter want a total accessor with a sentinel, and
; Axiom has no way to signal failure that does not either allocate (the
; builtin `Option` works, but every `data` value is heap-boxed, so an
; `Option`-returning getter allocates on every read) or abort the
; process. A caller that stores 0 as a real element and needs to tell it
; from "absent" must check `vecLen` first; that is the honest cost of
; this choice, and it is stated rather than hidden.
; RAW: a `Vec` carries no element type, so the word read back is
; whatever was pushed and the caller names it.
;@axiom:raw
(pub :: vecGet (-> Int Int a))

(pub fn (vecGet v i)
  (if (< i 0)
    (cast a 0)
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      (cast a 0)
      (memGetWord (vecData v) i)
    )
  )
)

; Overwrite the element at `i`. Returns the handle.
;
; Out-of-range writes are ignored rather than growing the vector: a
; caller that meant to append should say `vecPush`, and silently
; extending on a stray index turns an off-by-one into a slow leak instead
; of a wrong answer that `vecLen` makes visible.
(pub :: vecSet (-> Int Int a Int))

(pub fn (vecSet v i x)
  (if (< i 0)
    v
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      v
      {
        (memSetWord (vecData v) i x)
        v
      }
    )
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Growth
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Ensure `v` can hold `need` elements, reallocating if it cannot.
; Returns the handle.
(:: vecReserve (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (vecReserve v need)
  (if (<= need (vecCap v))
    v
    (vecReserveExactly v (vecGrownCap (vecCap v) need))
  )
)

; The first power-of-two multiple of `cap` that reaches `need`.
;
; Growth factor 2, not 1.5. The usual argument for 1.5 is that it lets an
; allocator reuse the blocks freed by earlier growth steps; with a bump
; allocator and no `free` there is nothing to reuse, so the only thing
; 1.5 buys is more reallocations and more leaked blocks. 2 keeps the
; total bytes copied across n pushes under 2n and the number of
; reallocations at log2(n).
;
; Terminates because `vecWithCapacity` guarantees `cap >= 1`.
(:: vecGrownCap (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (vecGrownCap cap need)
  (if (>= cap need)
    cap
    (vecGrownCap (* cap 2) need)
  )
)

; Move `v`'s elements into a fresh block of exactly `newCap` elements.
;
; Only `len` elements are copied, not `cap`: the words past `len` are
; unreachable through `vecGet`, so copying them would be pure cost. The
; new block comes from `memAlloc`, which zeroes, so the tail is 0 rather
; than whatever the arena last held there.
(:: vecReserveExactly (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (vecReserveExactly v newCap)
  (let ((bigger (memAlloc (* newCap 8))))
    {
      (memCopy bigger (vecData v) (* (vecLen v) 8))
      (memSetWord v 2 bigger)
      (memSetWord v 1 newCap)
      v
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stack operations
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Append `x`. Returns the handle - the same one, with this
; representation; see the module comment for why it is returned anyway.
;
; `vecData` is read *after* `vecReserve` runs, because reserving may have
; replaced the data pointer. Reading it into a `let` before the reserve
; would write the element into the abandoned block, and the bug would be
; invisible until the vector happened to grow.
(pub :: vecPush (-> Int a Int))

(pub fn (vecPush v x)
  (let ((len (vecLen v)))
    {
      (vecReserve v (+ len 1))
      (memSetWord (vecData v) len x)
      (memSetWord v 0 (+ len 1))
      v
    }
  )
)

; Remove and return the last element, or 0 if `v` is empty.
;
; 0-on-empty is ambiguous with a stored 0, for the reason given on
; `vecGet`. Callers that can push 0 must guard with `vecLen`.
;
; Capacity is not reduced: shrinking would reallocate and leak the larger
; block, so a vector used as a stack settles at its high-water mark and
; then stops allocating entirely, which is what a recursive-descent
; parser wants.
(pub :: vecPop (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecPop v)
  (let ((len (vecLen v)))
    (if (== len 0)
      0
      (let ((top (memGetWord (vecData v) (- len 1))))
        {
          (memSetWord v 0 (- len 1))
          top
        }
      )
    )
  )
)

; The last element without removing it, or 0 if `v` is empty.
(pub :: vecLast (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecLast v) (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1)))

; Drop every element, keeping the capacity. Returns the handle.
;
; The data block is deliberately not zeroed. Nothing can observe the
; stale words - `vecGet` and `vecSum` are both gated on `len` - so
; zeroing would be O(cap) of work to make a debugger's view prettier.
; Keeping the capacity is the point: a `Vec` reused as scratch across
; many iterations allocates once in total.
(pub :: vecClear (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecClear v) (memSetWord v 0 0))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Bulk reads
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The sum of every element.
;
; Exists so a test can check a whole vector's contents with one printed
; number instead of one per element. A sum is a weak check on its own -
; any bug that permutes elements passes - so tests pair it with indexed
; reads at the boundaries. `vecHash` is the position-sensitive companion
; for when order matters.
(pub :: vecSum (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecSum v) (vecSumFrom v 0 0))

(:: vecSumFrom (-> Int Int Int Int))

(fn (vecSumFrom v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    (vecSumFrom v (+ i 1) (+ acc (vecGet v i)))
  )
)

; A position-sensitive digest of the whole vector.
;
; `h * 31 + x` per element, reduced mod a prime small enough that
; `h * 31` cannot overflow `Int` (10^9 * 31 < 2^63). Unlike `vecSum` this
; changes when elements are reordered, which is what makes it usable as
; the single assertion in a test that cares about order.
(pub :: vecHash (-> Int Int))

(pub fn (vecHash v) (vecHashFrom v 0 1))

(:: vecHashFrom (-> Int Int Int Int))

(fn (vecHashFrom v i h)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    h
    (vecHashFrom v (+ i 1) (% (+ (* h 31) (vecGet v i)) 1000000007))
  )
)
